WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.950
A library chatbot leaked its own password.

2
00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.890
A student asked it to repeat its

3
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.610
instructions, and there, inside

4
00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.970
them, was the database password.

5
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.170
The developer had put it

6
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.050
there to save time.

7
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.420
Today: ten risks in apps built on

8
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.160
language models, and a defence for each.

9
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.400
OWASP is a non-profit community that

10
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.400
publishes free security guidance.

11
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.860
Its 2025 list for

12
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.240
language-model apps has ten risks.

13
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.670
Let's take them in three groups:

14
00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.910
what goes in, what comes out, and

15
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.290
the system around them.

16
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:42.230
What goes in.

17
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.100
Number one, prompt injection:

18
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.280
instructions hidden in input steer the

19
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:48.560
model.

20
00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.970
Number four, data and model poisoning: bad

21
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.990
training data plants wrong behaviour.

22
00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.300
Number eight, vector and embedding

23
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.240
weaknesses: a retrieval store that mixes

24
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.260
users' documents, or accepts poisoned

25
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:04.560
ones.

26
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:06.860
What comes out.

27
00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.640
Number two, sensitive information

28
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.120
disclosure: it reveals personal data or

29
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:13.420
secrets.

30
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.210
Number seven, system prompt

31
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.270
leakage, like our library bot.

32
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.670
Number five, improper output handling:

33
00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.970
its reply is pasted into a page, a

34
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.150
query or a command, and runs.

35
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.710
Number nine, misinformation:

36
00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:33.070
confident, wrong answers people act on.

37
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.370
Finally, the system around them.

38
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.970
Number three, the supply chain: models,

39
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.330
data and plug-ins made by others.

40
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.900
Number six, excessive agency: more tools

41
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.800
and permissions than the job needs.

42
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.080
Number ten, unbounded consumption:

43
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.060
one user's flood of requests runs up

44
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.020
the bill, or takes it down.

45
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:58.740
Quick check!

46
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.740
A chatbot's reply is pasted straight

47
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.220
into the course web page, and one

48
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.160
reply contains a piece of script.

49
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:08.090
Which risk is that?

50
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.940
Improper output handling.

51
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.760
The model's reply is text from a

52
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.020
source you don't control.

53
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.910
So treat it like any user input: escape

54
00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.330
it before HTML, use parameters for SQL,

55
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.350
never pass it to a shell.

56
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.940
Escaping turns special characters into

57
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.460
harmless codes, so the browser shows

58
00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.880
them instead of running them.

59
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.030
Do it in the wrong order, and you escape

60
00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.230
your own escapes: the page shows a mess.

61
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.960
The ampersand must go first.

62
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.680
And the library bot?

63
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.200
Never put secrets in a system

64
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.780
prompt: assume it will be read.

65
00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.710
Keep keys in the app, give the model

66
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.170
only this user's data, and check

67
00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.830
replies before they go out.

68
00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.240
In the app, a tiny model has memorised a

69
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.260
private line from its training data.

70
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.820
Ask it the right way and it

71
00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:05.860
repeats a card number.

72
00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.190
Add privacy noise and watch the leak

73
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:11.670
close, and the usefulness drop.

74
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:13.480
Your turn.

75
00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.410
A study assistant can delete files,

76
00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:18.950
but its job is only to read them.

77
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.520
Which risk is that?

78
00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.060
Excessive agency: take the

79
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:24.860
delete tool away.

80
00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.330
Remember: ten risks in three groups,

81
00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.950
model output is untrusted input, no

82
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.330
secrets in prompts, and the fewest

83
00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:35.450
tools the job needs.

84
00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.640
Try it tomorrow: go down the ten for an

85
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.980
LLM app you use or are building.

86
00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.470
For each, write one line: could it

87
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.950
happen here, and what stops it?

88
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.750
Then fix the fastest three.
